                "role": "assistant",
                "content": "**Notation**\n\n* \\(M\\) \u2013 mass of the hoop (radius \\(a\\)).\n* \\(m\\) \u2013 mass of the bead.\n* \\(X(t)\\) \u2013 horizontal coordinate of the centre of the hoop (the rail is taken as the \\(x\\)-axis).\n* \\(\\theta(t)\\) \u2013 polar angle of the bead measured from the lowest point of the hoop  \n  (\\(\\theta=0\\) means the bead is at the bottom).\n* \\(g\\) \u2013 magnitude of the gravitational acceleration (downward).\n\nThe bead\u2019s Cartesian coordinates are  \n\n\\[\nx = X + a\\sin\\theta ,\\qquad \ny = -a\\cos\\theta .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Lagrangian and conservation of \\(P_{X}\\)\n\nVelocities  \n\n\\[\n\\dot x = \\dot X + a\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta ,\\qquad \n\\dot y = a\\dot\\theta\\sin\\theta .\n\\]\n\nHence the kinetic energy of the bead is  \n\n\\[\nT_{\\text{bead}}\n      =\\frac12 m\\bigl[(\\dot X+a\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta)^2\n                     +(a\\dot\\theta\\sin\\theta)^2\\bigr]\n      =\\frac12 m\\Bigl[\\dot X^{\\,2}+2a\\dot X\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta\n                     +a^{2}\\dot\\theta^{\\,2}\\Bigr].\n\\]\n\nThe hoop translates as a rigid body, therefore  \n\n\\[\nT_{\\text{hoop}}=\\frac12 M\\dot X^{\\,2}.\n\\]\n\nThe only potential energy comes from the weight of the bead  \n\n\\[\nV = -mg\\,y = -mg(-a\\cos\\theta)= mga\\cos\\theta .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nL = T-V=\n\\frac12 (M+m)\\dot X^{\\,2}\n+ m a\\dot X\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta\n+\\frac12 m a^{2}\\dot\\theta^{\\,2}\n+ m g a\\cos\\theta } .\n\\]\n\nThe canonical momentum conjugate to \\(X\\),\n\n\\[\nP_{X}\\equiv\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\dot X}\n          =(M+m)\\dot X+m a\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta ,\n\\]\n\ndoes **not** appear explicitly in \\(L\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{dP_{X}}{dt}=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad P_{X}= {\\rm const.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   Elimination of \\(X\\) under the condition \\(P_{X}=0\\)\n\nSetting the total horizontal momentum to zero,\n\n\\[\n(M+m)\\dot X+m a\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta =0\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n\\dot X =-\\frac{m a}{M+m}\\,\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert (2.1) into the Lagrangian.  \nFirst compute the needed pieces:\n\n\\[\n\\dot X^{\\,2}= \\frac{m^{2}a^{2}}{(M+m)^{2}}\\dot\\theta^{\\,2}\\cos^{2}\\theta,\n\\qquad\nm a\\dot X\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta\n        =-\\frac{m^{2}a^{2}}{M+m}\\dot\\theta^{\\,2}\\cos^{2}\\theta .\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &=\n\\frac12 (M+m)\\dot X^{\\,2}\n+ m a\\dot X\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta\n+\\frac12 m a^{2}\\dot\\theta^{\\,2}\n+ m g a\\cos\\theta \\\\[2mm]\n&=\n\\frac12 m a^{2}\\dot\\theta^{\\,2}\n\\Bigl[1-\\frac{m}{M+m}\\cos^{2}\\theta\\Bigr]\n+ m g a\\cos\\theta .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nIt is convenient to write it as  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nL_{\\!{\\rm eff}}(\\theta,\\dot\\theta)\n=\n\\frac12 m a^{2}\\dot\\theta^{\\,2}\\,\n\\frac{M+m-m\\cos^{2}\\theta}{M+m}\n+ m g a\\cos\\theta } .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Equation of motion for \\(\\theta\\)\n\nApply the Euler\u2013Lagrange equation  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{{\\rm d}}{{\\rm d}t}\\!\\left(\n\\frac{\\partial L_{\\!{\\rm eff}}}{\\partial\\dot\\theta}\n\\right)-\\frac{\\partial L_{\\!{\\rm eff}}}{\\partial\\theta}=0 .\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nf(\\theta)=\\frac{M+m-m\\cos^{2}\\theta}{M+m},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{{\\rm d}f}{{\\rm d}\\theta}\n     =\\frac{2m\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}{M+m}\n     =\\frac{m\\sin2\\theta}{M+m}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial L_{\\!{\\rm eff}}}{\\partial\\dot\\theta}\n    = m a^{2}\\dot\\theta\\,f(\\theta),\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial L_{\\!{\\rm eff}}}{\\partial\\theta}\n    =\\frac12 m a^{2}\\dot\\theta^{\\,2}\\,f'(\\theta)-m g a\\sin\\theta .\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating the first term and inserting into the EL\u2011equation gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\bigl(M+m-m\\cos^{2}\\theta\\bigr)\\,\\ddot\\theta\n-3m\\dot\\theta^{\\,2}\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n+\\frac{M+m}{a}\\,g\\sin\\theta =0 } .\n\\]\n\nThis is the exact nonlinear equation governing the bead\u2019s angular motion when the centre of the hoop carries no net horizontal momentum.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   Small\u2011oscillation frequency about \\(\\theta=0\\)\n\nFor \\(|\\theta|\\ll1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\cos\\theta\\simeq 1-\\frac{\\theta^{2}}{2},\\qquad\n\\sin\\theta\\simeq\\theta,\\qquad\n\\sin2\\theta\\simeq2\\theta .\n\\]\n\nKeeping only the lowest non\u2011vanishing orders in the equation above:\n\n* The term \\(-3m\\dot\\theta^{\\,2}\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\) is of order \\(\\theta^{3}\\) (because \\(\\dot\\theta\\sim\\omega\\theta\\)), hence it can be dropped.\n* \\((M+m-m\\cos^{2}\\theta)\\approx M+m-m(1-\\theta^{2})=M+m\\theta^{2}\\approx M\\).\n\nThus the linearised equation reads  \n\n\\[\nM\\ddot\\theta+\\frac{M+m}{a}\\,g\\,\\theta\\simeq0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the angular frequency of small oscillations is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{(M+m)g}{M\\,a}} } .\n\\]\n\nWhen the bead is much lighter than the hoop (\\(m\\ll M\\)) this reduces to the familiar \\(\\omega=\\sqrt{g/a}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   Relation between \\(X\\) and \\(\\theta\\) for \\(P_{X}=0\\)\n\nFrom (2.1) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\dot X =-\\frac{m a}{M+m}\\cos\\theta\\,\\dot\\theta .\n\\]\n\nIntegrating with respect to time,\n\n\\[\nX(t)-X(t_{0})\n   =-\\frac{m a}{M+m}\\int_{t_{0}}^{t}\\cos\\theta\\,\\dot\\theta\\,dt\n   =-\\frac{m a}{M+m}\\bigl[\\sin\\theta-\\sin\\theta_{0}\\bigr],\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\theta_{0}=\\theta(t_{0})\\) and \\(X_{0}=X(t_{0})\\).\n\nIf at a particular instant the centre passes through the origin,\n\\(X_{0}=0\\) and \\(\\theta_{0}=\\theta_{0}\\) (given), the integration constant vanishes and we obtain the simple functional dependence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\displaystyle \nX(\\theta)=\\frac{m a}{M+m}\\,\\bigl(\\sin\\theta_{0}-\\sin\\theta\\bigr) } .\n\\]\n\nThus the horizontal displacement of the hoop is completely determined by the bead\u2019s angular position once the total horizontal momentum is zero.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Summary**\n\n| Item | Result |\n|------|--------|\n| Lagrangian | \\(L=\\frac12(M+m)\\dot X^{2}+ma\\dot X\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta+\\frac12 ma^{2}\\dot\\theta^{2}+mga\\cos\\theta\\) |\n| Conserved momentum | \\(P_{X}=(M+m)\\dot X+ma\\dot\\theta\\cos\\theta\\) |\n| Effective Lagrangian ( \\(P_{X}=0\\) ) | \\(L_{\\rm eff}= \\frac12 ma^{2}\\dot\\theta^{2}\\frac{M+m-m\\cos^{2}\\theta}{M+m}+mga\\cos\\theta\\) |\n| Exact \\(\\theta\\)\u2011equation | \\((M+m-m\\cos^{2}\\theta)\\ddot\\theta-3m\\dot\\theta^{2}\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta+\\frac{M+m}{a}g\\sin\\theta=0\\) |\n| Small\u2011oscillation frequency | \\(\\displaystyle\\omega=\\sqrt{\\frac{(M+m)g}{Ma}}\\) |\n| Relation \\(X(\\theta)\\) (zero momentum) | \\(\\displaystyle X(\\theta)=\\frac{ma}{M+m}\\bigl(\\sin\\theta_{0}-\\sin\\theta\\bigr)\\) |\n\nThese formulas completely describe the dynamics of the bead\u2013hoop system under the stated constraints.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer each part with derivations.\n\nSystem: hoop (mass M, radius a) free to slide horizontally on frictionless rail; bead (mass m) slides on hoop without friction. Coordinates given. Need Lagrangian: kinetic energy of hoop translation + rotation? Actually hoop's orientation fixed, so no rotational kinetic energy about its own axis (since it's rigid body moving translationally). The hoop mass M contributes translational KE: (1/2) M \\dot X^2 . Also bead has kinetic energy relative to ground: (1/2) m (\\dot x)^2 + (1/2) m (\\dot y)^2 . Potential energy: -m g y = -m g (-a cos\u03b8)= m g a cos\u03b8 . Also hoop may have potential? Hoop is uniform ring, its center height constant (rail is horizontal), so its potential energy constant (choose zero). So L = T - V.\n\nCompute velocities: x = X + a sin\u03b8 => \\dot x = \\dot X + a \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8 . y = -a cos\u03b8 => \\dot y = a \\dot \u03b8 sin\u03b8 .\n\nThus bead kinetic: (1/2) m [(\\dot X + a \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8)^2 + (a \\dot \u03b8 sin\u03b8)^2] = (1/2) m[ \\dot X^2 + 2 a \\dot X \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8 + a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 (cos^2\u03b8+ sin^2\u03b8)] = (1/2) m[ \\dot X^2 + 2 a \\dot X \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8 + a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 ].\n\nAdd hoop translational: (1/2) M \\dot X^2.\n\nTotal T = (1/2)(M+m)\\dot X^2 + m a \\dot X \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8 + (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2.\n\nPotential V = - m g a cos\u03b8 (plus constant).\n\nThus L = T - V = (1/2)(M+m)\\dot X^2 + m a \\dot X \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8 + (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 + m g a cos\u03b8.\n\nNow compute conjugate momentum P_X = \u2202L/\u2202\\dot X = (M+m)\\dot X + m a \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8.\n\nSince no explicit X dependence, dP_X/dt =0 \u2192 conserved.\n\nPart (2): Set P_X =0 => (M+m)\\dot X + m a \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8 =0 => \\dot X = -(m a /(M+m)) \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8.\n\nPlug into L eliminating X. Compute \\dot X term squared etc.\n\nFirst compute \\dot X^2 = (m^2 a^2/(M+m)^2) \\dot \u03b8^2 cos^2\u03b8.\n\nAlso term m a \\dot X \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8 = m a * (-(m a/(M+m)) \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8) * \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8 = - m^2 a^2/(M+m) \\dot \u03b8^2 cos^2\u03b8.\n\nNow substitute into L:\n\nL = (1/2)(M+m) \\dot X^2 + m a \\dot X \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8 + (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 + m g a cos\u03b8.\n\nReplace \\dot X^2 and product:\n\nTerm1: (1/2)(M+m)*(m^2 a^2/(M+m)^2) \\dot \u03b8^2 cos^2\u03b8 = (1/2) (m^2 a^2/(M+m)) \\dot \u03b8^2 cos^2\u03b8.\n\nTerm2: we already have - m^2 a^2/(M+m) \\dot \u03b8^2 cos^2\u03b8.\n\nCombine term1+term2: (1/2) -1 = -1/2 times (m^2 a^2/(M+m)) \\dot \u03b8^2 cos^2\u03b8.\n\nSo first two terms give -(1/2) (m^2 a^2/(M+m)) \\dot \u03b8^2 cos^2\u03b8.\n\nAdd third term (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2.\n\nThus total kinetic part becomes (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 - (1/2) (m^2 a^2/(M+m)) \\dot \u03b8^2 cos^2\u03b8 = (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 [1 - (m/(M+m)) cos^2\u03b8].\n\nSimplify factor: 1 - (m/(M+m)) cos^2\u03b8 = (M+m - m cos^2\u03b8)/(M+m).\n\nThus effective Lagrangian L_eff = (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (M+m - m cos^2\u03b8)/(M+m) + m g a cos\u03b8.\n\nAlternatively write L_eff = (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 \\frac{M+m - m \\cos^2\\theta}{M+m} + m g a \\cos\\theta.\n\nNow derive equation of motion via Euler-Lagrange: d/dt (\u2202L/\u2202\\dot \u03b8) = \u2202L/\u2202\u03b8.\n\nCompute \u2202L/\u2202\\dot \u03b8 = m a^2 \\dot \u03b8 * (M+m - m cos^2\u03b8)/(M+m).\n\nDerivative w.r.t t: m a^2 [(M+m - m cos^2\u03b8)/(M+m)] \\ddot \u03b8 - m a^2 \\dot \u03b8 * derivative of prefactor wrt \u03b8 times \\dot \u03b8.\n\nPrefactor f(\u03b8) = (M+m - m cos^2\u03b8)/(M+m). Derivative: df/d\u03b8 = (m * 2 cos\u03b8 sin\u03b8)/ (M+m) = (2m sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8)/(M+m) = (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m).\n\nThus d/dt (\u2202L/\u2202\\dot \u03b8) = m a^2 [(M+m - m cos^2\u03b8)/(M+m)] \\ddot \u03b8 - m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m).\n\nRight side \u2202L/\u2202\u03b8 = derivative of kinetic term plus potential term.\n\nKinetic term derivative: (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * derivative of prefactor f(\u03b8) = (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m).\n\nPotential term derivative: \u2202/\u2202\u03b8 (m g a cos\u03b8) = - m g a sin\u03b8.\n\nSet left minus right? Actually EL: d/dt (\u2202L/\u2202\\dot \u03b8) - \u2202L/\u2202\u03b8 =0.\n\nThus:\n\nm a^2 [(M+m - m cos^2\u03b8)/(M+m)] \\ddot \u03b8 - m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m) - [ (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m) - m g a sin\u03b8 ] =0.\n\nCombine terms: the kinetic derivative term includes - (m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m)). Subtracting the kinetic term derivative yields - (m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m)) - (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m) = - (3/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m). Wait sign careful: we have - (kinetic term derivative) - (potential derivative). But potential derivative is - m g a sin\u03b8, so subtracting gives + m g a sin\u03b8? Let's recompute: EL: d/dt (\u2202L/\u2202\\dot \u03b8) - \u2202L/\u2202\u03b8 =0.\n\n\u2202L/\u2202\u03b8 = (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m) - m g a sin\u03b8.\n\nThus subtract: d/dt(...) - [(1/2)... - m g a sin\u03b8]=0 => d/dt(...) - (1/2)... + m g a sin\u03b8 =0.\n\nHence equation:\n\nm a^2 [(M+m - m cos^2\u03b8)/(M+m)] \\ddot \u03b8 - m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m) - (1/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m) + m g a sin\u03b8 =0.\n\nCombine the two kinetic terms: - m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m) - (1/2) ... = - (3/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m).\n\nThus:\n\nm a^2 [(M+m - m cos^2\u03b8)/(M+m)] \\ddot \u03b8 - (3/2) m a^2 \\dot \u03b8^2 * (m sin2\u03b8)/(M+m) + m g a sin\u03b8 =0.\n\nDivide both sides by m a^2/(M+m):\n\n[(M+m - m cos^2\u03b8)] \\ddot \u03b8 - (3/2) m \\dot \u03b8^2 sin2\u03b8 + (M+m) g (sin\u03b8)/a =0.\n\nBut maybe there is simplification using identity sin2\u03b8 = 2 sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8.\n\nThus final exact equation:\n\n(M+m - m cos^2\u03b8) \\ddot \u03b8 - 3 m \\dot \u03b8^2 sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8 + (M+m) g (sin\u03b8)/a =0.\n\nThat's part (2).\n\nPart (3): Small oscillations about \u03b8=0. Expand for small \u03b8: cos\u03b8\u22481 - \u03b8^2/2, sin\u03b8\u2248\u03b8, sin2\u03b8\u22482\u03b8.\n\nPlug into equation linearized.\n\nFirst term coefficient: (M+m - m cos^2\u03b8) \u2248 (M+m - m (1 - \u03b8^2/2)^2 ) \u2248 (M+m - m (1 - \u03b8^2 + ...)) \u2248 (M+m - m + m \u03b8^2) = M + m \u03b8^2. For small \u03b8, leading term ~ M.\n\nSecond term: -3 m \\dot \u03b8^2 sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8 \u2248 -3 m \\dot \u03b8^2 \u03b8 (1) = -3 m \u03b8 \\dot \u03b8^2 (higher order O(\u03b8^3) due to product of \u03b8 and \\dot \u03b8^2?). Actually \\dot \u03b8^2 term multiplies \u03b8, giving order \u03b8^3 if \\dot \u03b8 ~ \u03c9 \u03b8, then term ~ \u03c9^2 \u03b8^3, negligible compared to linear term. So drop.\n\nThird term: (M+m) g (sin\u03b8)/a \u2248 (M+m) g \u03b8 / a.\n\nThus linearized equation: M \\ddot \u03b8 + (M+m) g \u03b8 / a \u22480.\n\nThus \u03c9^2 = (M+m) g /(M a). Therefore angular frequency \u03c9 = sqrt( (M+m) g / (M a) ).\n\nIf m << M, reduces to sqrt(g/a). Good.\n\nPart (4): With P_X=0, we have relation between X and \u03b8 from earlier: \\dot X = -(m a/(M+m)) \\dot \u03b8 cos\u03b8.\n\nIntegrate: X(t) - X(0) = -(m a/(M+m)) \u222b cos\u03b8 \\dot \u03b8 dt = -(m a/(M+m)) \u222b cos\u03b8 d\u03b8 = -(m a/(M+m)) sin\u03b8 + const.\n\nGiven initial condition at some instant: X=0, \u03b8=\u03b80. At that instant, define constant such that X=0 when \u03b8=\u03b80. So constant = (m a/(M+m)) sin\u03b80.\n\nThus general expression: X = -(m a/(M+m)) (sin\u03b8 - sin\u03b80).\n\nThus X as function of \u03b8: X = (m a/(M+m)) (sin\u03b80 - sin\u03b8).\n\nThat's answer.\n\nProvide full solution steps."
